A||N||8||grinthock||DAMN CAR! --- CONSTANT STALLING||||1||
Z||000000||grinthock||07-15-2001||12:32 AM||||OK, I have been having this problem for awile I REALLY hope someone can help...<P>The car has occasionally wanted to kill itself it's a 87GT Auto (The TCC is disconnected)<P>When I am not ON the gas and the car is running in such a gear that the revs come near 1,000 it decides WOW!! Let's just Choak and DIE!! The RPM dives down the the engine dies.  I have to manually restart... it did it a couple times, NOW ITS CONSTANT.....<P>I was thinking IAC, I havn't had a chance to hook up my laptop and look at the IAC, I am getting NO CODES.<P>I tried disconnecting the IAC, no difference, still doing it, and no codes STILL.<P>Any ideas?  BAD IAC?  Can they be CLEANED?  I am thinking I will remove it tommorow but right now it drives me nuts cause the car is stalling all hte time.  Keep in mind it's doing it EVEN IN NEUTRAL!  If I start the car it runs for 3-4 seconds then DIES.<P>Any help is appriciated and thanks in advance everyone!||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroSoul||07-15-2001||07:57 AM||||  Hey Grinny,<BR>  I think my car is headed in the same direction I am just a little behind you. I have been thinking it is the same thing. I have seen guys mention cleaning the IAC.  They say if that doesn't work replace it. I hope they aren't expensive. Let me know how yours comes out.||||reg||6
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||07-15-2001||08:26 AM||||Hey Grinthock!<BR>Three things I can think of checking, the EGR, TPS and MAP, they can go intermittant and cause this...<BR>If you want to clean the IAC properly, remove the throttle body and clean the manifold passage ways, also remove the EGR and clean the pintle at this time...<BR>The EGR is supposed to be closed at idle, but carbon buildup can keep the valve open and causes rough idle, and stalling...<BR>Course, when you get the valve clean and sealed, you could plug the hose barb for it, and run with out it to verify it was the cause...<BR>But be sure to reconnect it afterwards,as it is a part of your federal emmissions package...<P>Use an ohm meter to check the TPS, they go bad, and cause all kinds of problems, depending on how they fail...<BR>If they fail high, it is hard to impossible to start your car...<BR>If they fail low, then they stall at idle...<BR>If it fails at cruse, then the car will surge...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000003||grinthock||07-15-2001||08:33 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierowrecker:<BR><B>Hey Grinthock!<BR>Three things I can think of checking, the EGR, TPS and MAP, they can go intermittant and cause this...<BR>If you want to clean the IAC properly, remove the throttle body and clean the manifold passage ways, also remove the EGR and clean the pintle at this time...<BR>The EGR is supposed to be closed at idle, but carbon buildup can keep the valve open and causes rough idle, and stalling...<BR>Course, when you get the valve clean and sealed, you could plug the hose barb for it, and run with out it to verify it was the cause...<BR>But be sure to reconnect it afterwards,as it is a part of your federal emmissions package...<P>Use an ohm meter to check the TPS, they go bad, and cause all kinds of problems, depending on how they fail...<BR>If they fail high, it is hard to impossible to start your car...<BR>If they fail low, then they stall at idle...<BR>If it fails at cruse, then the car will surge...<BR>crash...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I doubt it's EGR because I would DEFINATLY get codes on that, it's not TPS, I tested it with my Laptop and the ECM is seeing proper TPS movement.....||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||07-15-2001||09:06 AM||||Your EGR valve could be stuck open and you would get no code. The code is sent from the EGR solenoid and if there are no vacume leaks  in the EGR system no code will register. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||BC-GT||07-15-2001||10:36 AM||||Could just be your fuel pump. Girlfriend just had the exact same symtoms as you describe. The shop had it for 3 days before it acted up again, replaced all kinds of stuff as well looking for it.<P>------------------<BR>Darryl Martin<BR>1985 GT <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/pf251a969c96964ef15691e97e7ac4489/feb64e3a.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||grinthock||07-15-2001||01:27 PM||||I would think if it was the Fuel Pump then other problems would arise and it woudln't be as flakey a problem as it is... Also, the stuck EGR, how can I check that||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fierowrecker||07-15-2001||01:35 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by grinthock:<BR><B> I doubt it's EGR because I would DEFINATLY get codes on that, it's not TPS, I tested it with my Laptop and the ECM is seeing proper TPS movement.....</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>When an EGR sticks open just a fraction, the code does not get set, but it does screw up the idle...<BR>To check the EGR, you need to remove it, slip a hose over the seat that protudes and blow...<BR>To clean it, lightly tap the pintle (pointy thing sticking out of the seat) till it stops shedding...<BR>It is clean when you can't blow through any more, just hope the seat isn't burnt...<P>Also the same with intermittant operation of the TPS, if it is good MOST of the time, then no code is set...<BR>I have found that it takes a hard failure to set a code most of the time...<P>According to my manual, they say;<BR>Check hot air tube is connected. (Not a factor)<BR>Check Thermac system operation.  (not a factor)<BR>Check for proper IAC operation.<BR>Check PCV valve operation.<BR>Check EGR for sticking open.<BR>Check A/C head pressure if it is too high.<BR>Check for plugged fuel lines.<BR>Check for weak spark...<P>That is directly out of the Pontiac service manual, and is usually a good place to START...<P>Also look for vacuume leaks, etc...<BR>crash...<p>[This message has been edited by Fierowrecker (edited 07-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Seanh||07-15-2001||01:55 PM||||I would think it might be MAP sersor related, from experience. When I built the engine, I broke the little clip that holds the plug in the MAP sensor, and sometimes now when I stomp the gas pedal and it moves the engine around, it comes unpluged. They are about $30 to replace if I remember correctly. Get some carb cleaner and spray it down the throttle body with intake hose off, and it should be enough to clean the IAC. <P>Might wanna check your distributor too. I broke the little reluctor in there, and it always died at idle, but never while moving in gear.||||reg||1
